From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 22:02:55 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: virtio_uml: kick every device but the console without a
 reply

With in-band notifications every kick was a round trip on the main
socket: the kicking CPU held sock_lock with its interrupts off until
the backend acknowledged the kick. A backend that reads the kick only
after the request it is running, such as a graphics submission that
runs for hundreds of milliseconds, kept that CPU off its interrupts for
as long, and every other CPU that kicked the device spun on the lock.
Under user-mode Linux every device interrupt is taken on CPU 0, so a
kick from CPU 0 also stalled every other device, sound among them.

A backend that is idle still has to be scheduled to read and answer a
kick, and when the host is busy that takes long. Measured with a vsock
stream, a kick from CPU 0 waited 0.3 to 1.5 ms on average and up to
209 ms, and 541 ms when the host starved the backend's thread; sent
without waiting it took 5 to 93 us, and bulk stream throughput rose by
20 to 28 percent.

Send the kick of every device but the console as a message that asks
for no reply. sock_lock still keeps it whole and out of another
request's round trip, ordering on the socket still puts it ahead of any
later request, and a backend that has gone still shows as a failed
send. The backend must accept a kick that asks for no acknowledgement,
as the protocol allows.

A console kick still waits for its acknowledgement and serves the
backend's requests meanwhile. The console driver spins on its output
queue with interrupts off after a kick, and its backend reads nothing
more until the guest has answered a configuration change it sent:
without the wait, a writer spinning on CPU 0 kept the interrupt that
answers from ever running. Every other driver sleeps, or takes its
buffers back from the queue's interrupt, while its device works.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c | 54 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 43 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c b/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c
index 61c5ef5b7f2f9b8a2c30a45235331818c74ed613..30390dbaac62913abbebc2f849ff2a279d77c45c 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
 #include <linux/string.h>
 #include <linux/virtio.h>
 #include <linux/virtio_config.h>
+#include <linux/virtio_ids.h>
 #include <linux/virtio_ring.h>
 #include <linux/time-internal.h>
 #include <linux/virtio-uml.h>
@@ -347,11 +348,12 @@ static int vhost_user_recv_req(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev,
  *
  * Only their order pairs a reply with its request, so nothing else may be
  * sent on the socket between the two: sock_lock covers the whole round trip.
- * In-band notifications make every kick such a round trip, sent from any CPU
- * and from atomic context, while another CPU may be reading the device's
- * configuration. A reply read after sock_lock was dropped could be taken by
- * that kick, which would then report a healthy backend as gone, and the
- * reader would take the kick's acknowledgement in its place.
+ * A console kick is such a round trip, and in-band notifications are sent from
+ * any CPU and from atomic context, while another CPU may be reading the
+ * device's configuration. A reply read after sock_lock was dropped could be
+ * taken by that kick, which would then report a healthy backend as gone, and
+ * the reader would take the kick's acknowledgement in its place. A kick that
+ * asks for no reply is kept whole and out of another request's round trip too.
  */
 static int vhost_user_round_trip(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev,
 				 struct vhost_user_msg *msg,
@@ -1050,6 +1052,36 @@ static int vhost_user_set_vring_state(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev,
 	return vhost_user_send(vu_dev, &msg, NULL, 0);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Notify the backend in-band that @index has new buffers.
+ *
+ * A kick asks for no acknowledgement. The kicking CPU holds sock_lock with
+ * its interrupts off, so a kick that waited would hold it for as long as the
+ * backend's thread takes to be scheduled and to run the queue, and a CPU 0
+ * held there takes no device interrupt meanwhile. A disconnect still shows
+ * here, as a failed send.
+ *
+ * A console kick alone waits for its acknowledgement, and serves the
+ * backend's requests while it waits (vhost_user_wait_resp()). The console
+ * driver spins on its output queue with interrupts off after a kick, and a
+ * backend may wait for the answer to a request of its own, such as a
+ * configuration change, before it reads the kick: a CPU 0 spinning there would
+ * never take the interrupt that answers. Every other driver sleeps, or takes
+ * its buffers back from the queue's interrupt, while its device works.
+ */
+static int vhost_user_kick_inband(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev, u32 index)
+{
+	struct vhost_user_msg msg = {
+		.header.request = VHOST_USER_VRING_KICK,
+		.header.size = sizeof(msg.payload.vring_state),
+		.payload.vring_state.index = index,
+	};
+
+	if (vu_dev->vdev.id.device == VIRTIO_ID_CONSOLE)
+		return vhost_user_send(vu_dev, &msg, NULL, 0);
+	return vhost_user_round_trip(vu_dev, &msg, NULL, 0, NULL, 0);
+}
+
 static int vhost_user_set_vring_num(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev,
 				    u32 index, u32 num)
 {
@@ -1143,13 +1175,13 @@ static bool vu_notify(struct virtqueue *vq)
 
 	if (info->kick_fd < 0) {
 		/*
-		 * An in-band notification is a synchronous round trip on the
-		 * main socket. Its transient failures are already retried or
-		 * waited out inside that round trip, so anything it reports
-		 * here is a genuine transport failure.
+		 * An in-band notification is a message on the main socket,
+		 * and a console's alone a synchronous round trip. Its
+		 * transient failures are already retried or waited out inside
+		 * it, so anything it reports here is a genuine transport
+		 * failure.
 		 */
-		rc = vhost_user_set_vring_state(vu_dev, VHOST_USER_VRING_KICK,
-						vq->index, 0);
+		rc = vhost_user_kick_inband(vu_dev, vq->index);
 		if (!rc)
 			return true;
 	} else {
